    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/0/80547.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "It is at a very important start, but no. We have to do everything. And I think that's been part of the dilemma is that we've spent so much time trying to figure out, you know, one thing. We are in a crisis. Climate change is real. We really have passed. I mean, scientists that threshold were telling everyday Americans and reporting to Congress in the 60s. that we were in trouble just in that context of using gasoline and the number of cars on the road in the 60s. This is how we ended up with small reductions, 60s, 70s, and 80s. And they made a huge difference, but we focused on one thing in one way in a couple of states in particular that had large population centers. And a lot of driving, like California and Los Angeles, we didn't think about this as a national, international agenda. So I do think we are now clear that it is an international agenda and that the entire globe is going to have to do this on every single front. So the methane rules and the no flaring requirements, even making sure that we're recycling water, that we want renewable energy, not diesel engines, powering our wells and all of the work going on at each drill site, closing up abandoned wells. All of these and more are critical. for making sure that we're reducing and minimizing all of our emissions in oil and gas. Now we got to figure out transportation, agriculture, we've got to have lots of conversations and significant policies on consumption. Americans are some of the, I think we are the highest consumers of energy per person in the world and in the industrialized nation. That's not sustainable. I think the innovation here can be very productive for a state like New Mexico. So, you know, we're meeting our targets for renewable energy. We have the largest wind. farm in North America. We are the cheapest place for solar. We've got more than 12,000 jobs in solar. We added more jobs in solar last year than any other state. I mean, we're doing all the things that create those transitions. We need to do more. And I'm very excited, actually, again, about the Relationship Reduction Act, because we're $370 billion. $20 billion when you add the extra five that are US senators got in the legislation over the finish line for water. We're going to need all of it and thinking about how to get carbon out of the atmosphere. I'm thinking about other cleaner ways to have access to energy, to maybe doubling our efforts and knowing that we can meet those targets. I want New Mexico to be net zero by 2050. I have to have a lot of strategies in play to do that. It can't just be where, right, by 2045 utilities, 2050 rural co-ops. I think we might beat that 2050 actually by all of our partners. But we're going to have to do a lot more. And so you should expect us to have, again, we've had very robust investments in environmental policies and incentives, including clean car standards and a climate change task force, making sure that we're meeting and joining the U.S. Climate Alliance, that we're going to meet the Paris Accords. And as you know, New Mexico has been identified as a leading sub-national, which just means you may not be the president of the United States, but as a state in and of yourself, you're leading the world in policies. It can really make a difference. We're going to need more trees and more healthy, right? Good soil to help us draw carbon out of the atmosphere, all of the above.",
